  "name": "FACTOR - V  LEIDEN MUTATION SCREENING (F5 - G1691A)",
  "code": "2336",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<ul>\n<li>A \"Detected\" result indicates presence of mutation.</li>\n<li>A \"Not-Detected\" result indicates absence of mutation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>&nbsp;<strong>Clinical Background:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Factor V Leiden (FVL) thrombophilia is characterized by a poor anticoagulant response to activated protein C (APC) and an increased risk for venous thromboembolism (VTE).</li>\n<li>The most common clinical manifestations of APC resistance caused by the FVL mutation are venous thrombosis and pulmonary embolism.</li>\n<li>Among patients with venous thromboembolism, FVL occurs in 20% of all cases and in up to 50% of selected patients with thrombophilia.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Factor V Leiden&nbsp;thrombophilia&nbsp;<strong>should be suspected</strong>&nbsp;in individuals with the following</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A history of first and recurrent venous thromboembolism (VTE) manifest as deep vein thrombosis (DVT) or pulmonary embolism (PE), especially in women with a history of VTE during pregnancy or in association with use of estrogen-containing contraceptives.</li>\n<li>A family history of recurrent thrombosis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Limitation of Assay :</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>PCR is a highly sensitive technique; common reasons for paradoxical results are contamination during specimen collection, selection of inappropriate specimen and inherent PCR inhibitors in the sample.</li>\n</ul>",
